NAME
    cores - list the libretro cores EmuSen can download and those
    installed, describe one, and fetch libretro's catalogue again

SYNOPSIS
    cores ls [--installed|--available|--updates|--unsupported] [<console>]
    cores info <core>
    cores fetch
    cores wait [cores<n>]

DESCRIPTION
    The command of the emusen.cores module, the shell's face of the core
    tool; the Cores window is its other face, over the same jobs. It
    reaches the tool through the emusen.library service, version 1.5 or
    later, whose requests beginning core- the platform library hands to
    the core tool.

    A core is named by its file's stem: pcsx2 for pcsx2_libretro.so. The
    stem names the core's folder under /Cores/libretro and its files; the
    core's own corename (LRPS2 for pcsx2) is shown, and names nothing.

    NOTHING IS FETCHED UNLESS ASKED. cores fetch is the only network
    request: it reads libretro's core information archive
    (assets/frontend/info.zip) and this platform's nightly index
    (nightly/linux/x86_64/latest/.index-extended) from
    buildbot.libretro.com over HTTPS, the certificate checked against the
    system's own store, a redirect to any other host refused. Both are
    checked before the cached copies in /Cores/libretro/.catalogue/ are
    replaced, so a fetch that fails leaves the catalogue as it was. cores
    ls and cores info read the cached copies, and work offline.

    cores ls prints one line per core: state, console, id, source,
    version, size and licence, separated by spaces, an empty field as -
    and spaces inside a field as _, so that awk and cut can take them
    apart; the licence is the rest of the line. The states:

        installed     downloaded, and its index line unchanged
        update        downloaded, and the index offers another build
        available     in the catalogue, not downloaded
        unsupported   EmuSen's host cannot run it: a core needing only
                      OpenGL or Direct3D, or one for a system no EmuSen
                      console runs; cores info gives the reason
        added         a folder under /Cores/libretro without a NOTICE,
                      put there by the player; never removed by Cores

    Whether an installed core is approved, waits for approval, or needs
    refreshing after EmuSen was updated is the runtime's trust, shown in
    the Cores window.

    cores info prints what the window's detail pane shows, as key: value
    lines: the names and authors, the licence, the version and build,
    the systems, the notes, the permissions, the firmware it names (the
    player's own, which nothing fetches), and where the build comes from
    with the index's CRC-32; for an installed core also its SHA-256 and
    the date it was fetched.

CONSOLES
    A core is placed under EmuSen's consoles by its information's
    database names, or its systemid where those are blank. Until the
    systems table's libretro names reach this build, the console field
    is - and no core is called unsupported for its system.

JOBS
    Each command is a job of the core tool. One the line's budget cuts
    short goes on in the background as cores<n>: cores wait follows it,
    ps lists it, kill stops it.

EXAMPLES
    cores fetch
    cores ls --available
    cores ls --updates | wc -l
    cores ls --installed | grep GPL
    cores info pcsx2
    for c in $(cores ls --updates | awk '{print $3}'); do cores info $c; done

SOURCES
    libretro, "buildbot.libretro.com nightly linux x86_64",
    https://buildbot.libretro.com/nightly/linux/x86_64/latest/ and its
    .index-extended, read 2026-10-31: one line per core zip, its date,
    the zip's CRC-32 and its name.
    libretro, "libretro-core-info", 00_example_libretro.info,
    https://github.com/libretro/libretro-core-info, read 2026-10-31: the
    keys of a core information file.

